    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT & PRINCIPLE:\nActivation energy and temperature influence on redox titration rate.\n\n2. GIVEN INFORMATION / DATA:\nTitration: $2MnO_4^- + 5C_2O_4^{2-} + 16H^+ \\rightarrow 2Mn^{2+} + 10CO_2 + 8H_2O$.\n\n3. FORMULA / EQUATION:\nHigh activation energy barrier for cleavage of $C-C$ bond in oxalate.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP REASONING & CALCULATION:\n1. The reaction between permanganate ($MnO_4^-$) and oxalate ($C_2O_4^{2-}$) involves breaking the strong carbon-carbon bond in oxalic acid.\n2. At room temperature, this chemical reaction has a high activation energy ($E_a$), making the initial rate imperceptibly slow.\n3. Heating the mixture to $60-70^\\circ\\text{C}$ provides the necessary thermal activation energy to initiate the reaction.\n4. Once the first drop of $MnO_4^-$ reacts, $Mn^{2+}$ ions are generated, which act as an autocatalyst, accelerating subsequent decolorization.\n5. (Heating above $70^\\circ\\text{C}$ is avoided to prevent thermal decomposition of oxalic acid into $CO, CO_2$, and $H_2O$).\n\n5. FINAL CONCLUSION:\nTo increase the rate of reaction, because the reaction between $\\text{KMnO}_4$ and oxalic acid is very slow at room temperature\n\n6. OPTION ANALYSIS & COMMON TRAPS:\nHeating does not prevent $KMnO_4$ decomposition; decomposing oxalic acid beforehand would ruin the analytical titration.",
