    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT & PRINCIPLE:\nFerric chloride test for aromatic phenolic groups.\n\n2. GIVEN INFORMATION / DATA:\nPhenol + freshly neutralized $FeCl_3$ aqueous solution.\n\n3. FORMULA / EQUATION:\n$6C_6H_5OH + FeCl_3 \\rightarrow [Fe(OC_6H_5)_6]^{3-} \\text{ (violet complex)} + 3H^+ + 3HCl$\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP REASONING & CALCULATION:\n1. Phenol reacts with neutral ferric chloride solution to form a coordination complex anion: $[Fe(OC_6H_5)_6]^{3-}$.\n2. Phenolate ions coordinate to the central iron(III) ion as bidentate/monodentate ligands, enabling ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) transitions.\n3. This electronic charge transfer absorbs specific wavelengths of visible light, producing a vivid violet or purple coloration.\n4. Aliphatic alcohols do not give this test because their alkoxide ions are not sufficiently acidic to form stable complexes with $Fe^{3+}$ in neutral aqueous medium.\n\n5. FINAL CONCLUSION:\nViolet / Purple\n\n6. OPTION ANALYSIS & COMMON TRAPS:\nBlood red coloration is given by thiocyanate ($SCN^-$) with $Fe^{3+}$; Prussian blue is the Lassaigne nitrogen test.",
